## Two identifiers, doing two different jobs

Conflating these is the central trap.

**`correlationId`** (the MID) is the source event identity. It is the only key the dedupe store uses. It answers "have I seen this PWSSP event before?"

**`workId`** is the advice identity. Everything downstream of the fan-out keys on it. It answers "have I already produced this specific advice?"

Legitimate fan-out produces distinct `workId`s under a shared `correlationId`, so it cannot be collapsed. Reprocessing produces byte-identical `workId`s, so it can be. Both requirements are satisfied at once.

**The `workId` must derive from a stable business discriminator** — preference type, recipient, channel, whatever actually distinguishes the advices — and never from the ordinal position in the fan-out. If you hash the index and the mapping logic later changes emission order, a replay produces different `workId`s for the same advices and the idempotency guarantee silently disappears. This is worth being pedantic about at design time because the failure is invisible until it matters.

### Open question

Does PWSSP carry a genuinely unique, producer-stable event id? If not, the dedupe key is a hash of a canonical form of the business payload with volatile fields excluded — ingest timestamps, trace headers. That canonical form needs to be agreed explicitly and documented, not left to whoever writes the hash function.

