## Approach 1 (recommended) — durable work topic, worker owns the advice end to end

Kafka Streams plans the work and stops. A Spring Kafka listener calls PCSS, finishes the advice, and produces to `PANTS_PROCESSED`. Both run in the same Spring Boot application, in separate poll loops.

### Stage 1 — Streams planner, EOS v2

Consume PWSSP. Filter. Deduplicate against a keyed state store. Perform the deterministic field and address mappings, derive account and office, then explode into N advice work items and write them to `PWSSP_PCSS_WORK`. Each work item carries the derived payload, a declaration of how many PCSS lookups it needs (0, 1, or 2), the `correlationId`, and the `workId`.

The dedupe store needs a TTL and a wall-clock punctuator that evicts old keys, sized to your realistic replay window. Unbounded dedupe stores are one of the most common ways Streams applications die in production.

### Stage 2 — Spring Kafka worker, transactional

A `@KafkaListener` on a separate consumer group reads `PWSSP_PCSS_WORK`, performs the zero, one, or two PCSS GETs, applies preference-specific address mapping, adds the preference and engine headers, runs business validation, and produces to `PANTS_PROCESSED`.

Because it is a different poll loop, PCSS latency has no relationship to the Streams poll interval. That isolation is the entire point.

### Sequence

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant UP as Upstream
    participant SRC as PWSSP_PDM_PUBLIC
    participant S1 as Streams stage 1 (EOS v2)
    participant DD as Dedupe store
    participant WT as PWSSP_PCSS_WORK
    participant W as Advice worker (listener)
    participant API as PCSS API
    participant OUT as PANTS_PROCESSED
    participant ERR as PANTS_ADVICE_ERROR

    UP->>SRC: PWSSP event, correlationId = MID
    SRC->>S1: consume, EOS transaction opens
    S1->>S1: filter unwanted records
    S1->>DD: get(correlationId)
    alt already seen
        DD-->>S1: present
        S1->>S1: drop, advance offset only
    else new source event
        DD-->>S1: absent
        S1->>DD: put(correlationId, seenAt)
        S1->>S1: field and address mapping, derive account and office
        S1->>S1: fan out to N advices, workId per advice from business discriminator
        S1->>WT: produce N work items, key = account
    end
    Note over S1,WT: One transaction commits input offsets, dedupe changelog and work records together
    Note over S1,DD: Wall-clock punctuator evicts MIDs past the replay window

    WT->>W: consume with read_committed, transaction opens
    opt lookupsRequired is 1 or 2
        W->>API: GET account
        API-->>W: 200
    end
    opt lookupsRequired is 2
        W->>API: GET office
        API-->>W: 200
    end
    W->>W: preference address mapping, preference and engine headers, business validation
    W->>OUT: produce advice, key = workId
    Note over W,OUT: Transaction commits the advice and the consumer offsets together

    alt transient failure, single timeout or 5xx
        W->>API: 2 to 3 retries, jittered backoff, capped inside max.poll.interval.ms
    else sustained outage, circuit breaker opens
        W->>W: pause the listener container
        loop probe every 30s until healthy
            W->>API: health probe, half open
        end
        W->>W: resume from the same offset
        Note over WT,W: Offsets never advanced. Backlog waits in the work topic. PWSSP is not reingested.
    else permanent, 400 or 422 or validation reject
        W->>ERR: produce with reasonCode, correlationId, workId
    end
```

### Failure handling — three tiers, and the tiers matter

**Transient** — a single timeout, an isolated 5xx. Bounded in-memory retry with jittered exponential backoff, two or three attempts over a few seconds, capped well inside `max.poll.interval.ms`.

**Sustained** — a Resilience4j circuit breaker wraps the PCSS client. When it opens, pause the listener container via `KafkaListenerEndpointRegistry` rather than retrying or dead-lettering. A scheduled probe drives half-open, and on recovery the container resumes.

During the outage no offsets advance, unprocessed work sits durably in the work topic, and consumption picks up exactly where it stopped. This is the direct answer to the hour-long-outage requirement: nothing is lost, nothing is dead-lettered en masse, PWSSP is never reingested, and recovery is automatic with no operator action.

Set work topic retention comfortably above the maximum tolerable outage — seven days, not one. Do not compact it; it is a queue, not a table.

**Permanent** — 400, 422, business validation rejections, mapping failures. Straight to an error topic with a reason code, never retried.

The one genuinely ambiguous case is 404. Decide deliberately whether "account not found" means the data is bad or means PCSS has not caught up yet. If the latter, a short delayed-retry topic ahead of the error topic is justified. Get that answer from the PCSS team rather than guessing.

### Why not `@RetryableTopic`

Spring's non-blocking retry chain is the right tool when failures are per-record and independent. For a whole-dependency outage it converts a clean pause into thousands of records fanned across retry topics with ordering destroyed and a messy reconciliation afterwards. Pause-and-resume is simpler and preserves per-partition ordering.

### Exactly-once, and why the REST caveat matters less than it sounds

The stated constraint is that an external REST API cannot participate in a Kafka transaction. True, but PCSS is a GET — it has no side effects.

So run the worker as a transactional read-process-write: transactional producer, offsets sent to the transaction, consumer on `read_committed`.

- Hop one is atomic under Streams EOS v2 — input offsets, dedupe changelog, and work topic output commit together.
- Hop two is atomic under the Spring transaction.

The only non-transactional element in the entire path is an idempotent read. On replay it costs a duplicate lookup and nothing else. You get effectively-once end to end, not at-least-once with dedup bolted on.

Two caveats:

- Downstream consumers of `PANTS_PROCESSED` must read with `read_committed` or the guarantee is decorative.
- Ship the `workId` on the output record, as key or header, and ask downstream to be idempotent on it. EOS does not survive offset resets, topic recreation, or manual replays from the error topic, and all three will happen eventually.

### Partitioning the work topic

Key by **account or office** for cache locality on PCSS responses plus per-entity ordering, at the risk of skew if one account dominates volume. Key by **`workId`** for even spread and no ordering.

Start with the account key and instrument partition-level lag. The caching benefit is real — many advices in a batch will share accounts, and a Caffeine cache in front of PCSS keyed on account cuts call volume substantially.

### Zero-lookup advices

Route them through the work topic anyway rather than short-circuiting from Streams to `PANTS_PROCESSED`. The direct path saves a few milliseconds and costs two code paths, two ordering regimes, and two places to reproduce a bug.

### Why there is no result topic or stage 2

An earlier shape of this design routed PCSS results back through a result topic into a second Streams stage. That costs a topic, a stage, and one hard problem: an advice needing two lookups produces two result records that stage 2 must correlate. That means another state store keyed by `workId` accumulating partial results, plus a punctuator to time out advices whose second result never arrives.

That is a second scheduler — the exact thing choosing this approach was meant to avoid. It also requires threading the full advice payload through the request and result records, or stashing it in yet another store.

Collapsing it makes two lookups two sequential calls inside one listener invocation. No correlation, no aggregation store, no timeout logic. The topology drops from four boxes to two.

