## Approach 2 (viable at this volume) — single Streams app, pending work in a state store

One application. Pending PCSS work lives in a second state store instead of a topic, and a punctuator drains it.

Drawn below as **synchronous and serial with a bounded tick**, which is what 200K/day justifies. The async worker-pool variant is the harder version and buys throughput that is not needed here — see the note at the end of this section if you go that way regardless.

### Sequence

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant UP as Upstream
    participant SRC as PWSSP_PDM_PUBLIC
    participant ST as Streams app (processor and punctuator)
    participant DD as Dedupe store
    participant PW as Pending work store and due index
    participant API as PCSS API
    participant OUT as PANTS_PROCESSED
    participant ERR as PANTS_ADVICE_ERROR

    UP->>SRC: PWSSP event, correlationId = MID
    SRC->>ST: consume
    ST->>ST: filter unwanted records
    ST->>DD: get(correlationId)
    DD-->>ST: absent, new source event
    ST->>DD: put(correlationId, seenAt)
    ST->>ST: mapping, derive account and office, fan out to N advices
    ST->>PW: put(workId, payload, status PENDING, attempt 0, dueAt now, dispatchedAt null)
    Note over ST,PW: EOS commits input offsets and both changelogs together

    loop Wall-clock punctuator every 10s, never stream time
        ST->>PW: sweep IN_FLIGHT items whose dispatchedAt is older than the stale timeout
        PW-->>ST: orphans left by a crash or a rebalance
        ST->>PW: reset orphans to PENDING
        alt circuit breaker open
            ST->>ST: return immediately, dispatch nothing
        else breaker closed or half open
            ST->>PW: scan due index, take up to K items
            PW-->>ST: due work items
            loop per item, until K items or T ms spent
                ST->>PW: set IN_FLIGHT, dispatchedAt now
                ST->>API: GET account, then office if required, synchronous
                API-->>ST: 200 or 5xx or timeout
                alt success
                    ST->>ST: address mapping, headers, business validation
                    ST->>OUT: forward advice with explicit correlationId and workId headers
                    ST->>PW: delete(workId)
                else retryable
                    ST->>PW: status PENDING, attempt++, dueAt now plus backoff
                else permanent
                    ST->>ERR: forward with reasonCode
                    ST->>PW: delete(workId)
                end
            end
        end
        ST->>ST: return unconditionally so poll runs, emit backlogDepth gauge
    end
```

### Five pieces that are not optional

A status field gives you durable bookkeeping. It does not give you a retry runtime. "We're already tracking status, so retries are handled" is the easy 10% of the problem.

**1. Wall clock, not stream time.** Stream time only advances when records arrive. After 17:00 the input goes quiet, and with `STREAM_TIME` punctuation the scheduler never fires — exactly when the backlog is meant to be draining overnight. This is a silent, total failure of the retry mechanism at the worst possible moment. Use `PunctuationType.WALL_CLOCK_TIME`.

**2. Dispatch timestamp and stale sweeper.** Crash mid-call, restore from the changelog, and an `IN_FLIGHT` record has no call in flight and no owner — the HTTP client that owned it no longer exists. Without a `dispatchedAt` timestamp and a sweep that resets stale `IN_FLIGHT` back to `PENDING`, those records strand permanently. The same happens on partition migration during a rebalance. This is the single most common bug in hand-rolled versions of this pattern and it is invisible until the first production restart during a backlog.

**3. Circuit breaker.** Without one, every tick during an outage dispatches the whole due set at a dead PCSS, all of them fail, and every attempt counter increments. That is a retry storm aimed at a service that is already down. Status tracking does not prevent it; it only records it happening.

**4. Explicit headers on forward.** A punctuator has no input record, so timestamps and headers are not inherited. `correlationId` and `workId` must be set by hand on every forward or traceability breaks.

**5. Backlog gauge and eviction.** Work in a topic shows up as consumer lag on dashboards and alerts you already have. Work in RocksDB shows up nowhere unless you build a custom gauge. During an incident, "how deep is the hole and is it draining" is the only question anyone will ask. Terminal entries also need deleting or expiring, or the store grows without bound.

### The bounded tick

Since the punctuator runs on the StreamThread, each tick must process at most K items or at most T milliseconds and then return unconditionally so `poll()` runs.

Two consequences to price in:

- Throughput ceiling becomes K items per punctuation interval per partition. K and T become production tuning knobs with non-obvious interactions that nobody will remember the reasoning for in six months.
- Latency floor is roughly half the punctuation interval even when PCSS is healthy and the queue is empty, because nothing moves until the next tick.

### Finding due items

Keyed by `workId`, finding due items means a full range scan every punctuation — over a backlog that is largest exactly when you are most behind. The alternative is a second store keyed by `dueAt`, kept consistent with the first. Both writes land in the same transaction so consistency is guaranteed, but it is another store to restore and another thing to get wrong.

Every attempt-count increment is a write to the primary store plus a delete and insert in the index, all of which hit the changelog. Write amplification peaks during the outage.

### If you keep the async worker pool

The completion path is the dangerous part. **Never touch the state store or call `forward()` from the HTTP completion thread.** State stores are not thread-safe, and forwarding from a foreign thread corrupts the topology in ways that surface as bizarre, non-reproducible errors.

The worker pushes onto a concurrent completion queue. The *next* punctuation drains it and performs the store writes and the forwards. Nothing touches the store except the StreamThread. This is mandatory structure, not a detail.

An uncaught exception in `punctuate()` propagates up and kills the StreamThread, so PCSS error handling must be comprehensive and inside the punctuator.

### Restoration

During an outage the store accumulates the backlog. If a node then restarts or a rebalance fires, RocksDB must restore that backlog from the changelog before processing resumes — precisely when the system is already behind. With a work topic, a reassigned consumer just starts reading from an offset: no restoration, no warm-up. Standby replicas mitigate this at the cost of memory and disk on every node.

At 22K entries this is seconds, not minutes, so it is a minor concern at current volume. It scales badly.

