## Comparison

| | Approach 1 — work topic | Approach 2 — state store |
|---|---|---|
| Deployables | One (two poll loops in one app) | One |
| Where pending work lives | Durable work topic | RocksDB + changelog (also a topic) |
| Retry, backoff, DLT | Configuration | Hand-written |
| Backpressure during outage | Container pause and resume | Hand-written breaker + skip logic |
| Backlog visibility | Standard consumer lag | Custom gauge only |
| Restart with backlog | Read from offset | Restore RocksDB from changelog |
| Enrichment concurrency | Independent of input partitions | Welded to input partition count |
| Throughput ceiling | Scales by adding partitions to work topic | ~26 advices/sec at six partitions, serial |
| Code you own | Dedupe, mapping, validation | Dedupe, mapping, validation, scheduler, sweeper, breaker, index, eviction, completion queue |

Both use a state store for source-event dedup — that is a keyed lookup against recent history, exactly the access pattern RocksDB is built for. They differ only in whether the *work queue* also lives there, which is a queue access pattern, and Kafka topics are very good queues.

"State store instead of a topic" is really "one topic, plus RocksDB, plus a hand-rolled queue runtime, instead of one topic." A changelog is a topic, so the infrastructure is not avoided — only the tooling around it.

## Recommendation

**Approach 1**, with both stages in a single Spring Boot application.

At 200K/day the performance difference is irrelevant and the maintenance difference is not. Approach 1 gets backoff, concurrency limiting, pause-and-resume, and dead-lettering from configuration; Approach 2 gets a status field and you write the scheduler around it. Since both now run as one deployable, avoiding a second artifact is no longer a reason to prefer the state store.

Approach 2 is a legitimate choice if adding a topic is a real organisational cost — approval workflows, naming standards, capacity review. If it is a few lines of Terraform, take the topic.

**Explicitly rejected:** synchronous PCSS calls inside the Streams processor, with or without blocking retry. Under a sustained outage it either stalls past the poll interval into a rebalance storm, or fails fast to a DLT and forces manual replay — which directly violates the "resumes automatically without reingestion" requirement.

---

## Worth checking before building either

Ask whether PCSS can publish a change feed for the account and office reference data. If it can, mirror it into a compacted topic, join it as a `GlobalKTable`, and the synchronous REST call disappears from the hot path entirely — along with the timeouts, the circuit breaker, the work topic, and most of this document.

That would beat both designs above by a wide margin. It only fails if PCSS data is genuinely request-specific or too large to mirror, so it is worth five minutes with the PCSS owners before building any of this.

## Open questions

1. Does PWSSP carry a producer-stable unique event id, or must the dedupe key be a canonical hash? If the latter, which fields are in the canonical form?
2. Does PCSS 404 mean bad data (permanent) or replication lag (retryable)?
3. Does "advices can go next day" have a hard downstream cutoff — dispatch batch, regulatory clock — that is the real deadline?
4. What is the actual hourly arrival distribution within the 08:00–17:00 window?
5. Can PCSS publish account and office reference data as a change feed?
